            "pages": {
              "end": 9,
              "start": 9
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Bryan A. Stevenson, Esq., on behalf of the Petitioner, petitioner\u2019s counsel argues that children are categorically prohibited from being subjected to life\u2011without\u2011parole sentences."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Mr. Stevenson",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "981ed7c7c3d7bb166ea5949d5233cb7a64a02375211d22a0eabbd31a4cbc8c2f",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-981ed7c7c3d7-0001-0000"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 21,
              "start": 21
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Mr. Stevenson, petitioner also points out that psychologists say long\u2011term judgments about rehabilitation of young people are unreliable."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "John C. Neiman, Jr.",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "981ed7c7c3d7bb166ea5949d5233cb7a64a02375211d22a0eabbd31a4cbc8c2f",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-981ed7c7c3d7-0001-0002"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 28,
              "start": 28
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to John C. Neiman, Jr., according to John C. Neiman, Jr., respondent\u2019s counsel argues that life\u2011without\u2011parole for aggravated murder offenders like Evan Miller aligns with national consensus, is morally justified, and serves legitimate penological goals."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Court",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "981ed7c7c3d7bb166ea5949d5233cb7a64a02375211d22a0eabbd31a4cbc8c2f",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-981ed7c7c3d7-0001-0003"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 30,
              "start": 29
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Thirty\u2011nine jurisdictions allow life\u2011without\u2011parole for juveniles, and twenty\u2011seven of them make it the statutory minimum sentence."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Court",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "981ed7c7c3d7bb166ea5949d5233cb7a64a02375211d22a0eabbd31a4cbc8c2f",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-981ed7c7c3d7-0003-0000"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 57,
              "start": 57
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Studies show juveniles, especially homicide offenders, are less likely to recidivate than many adult offenders."
      }
    ],
